  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Increasing chances for rain this evening for SE \nWV and for areas north of Highway 460 overnight tonight. \nWidespread rain and low clouds for the entire area Saturday and \nSunday.\n\nAs dusk falls this evening, a slow-moving cold front will \ngradually approach from the west. Isolated showers will begin \ntracking across southeastern West Virginia over the next few \nhours, while lingering dry air keeps conditions mostly clear and\ndry across southwestern Virginia and northwestern North \nCarolina. Later tonight, rain coverage will steadily expand \nnorth of Highway 460. By Saturday morning, rainfall will become \nmore widespread across the northern half of the region as the \nboundary shifts closer.\n\nAs a new shortwave ejects out of western Canada tomorrow \nmorning, it will dig farther south and support rain across a \nmuch broader area. The surface front will stall near the North \nCarolina and South Carolina border by tomorrow afternoon. By \nthat time, a cold air damming pattern will take hold, and warm \nair overrunning the cooler surface layer will keep showers in \nthe forecast, with a storm or two potentially developing along \nthe western and far southern counties.\n\nRain chances will peak on Sunday alongside the passage of a \nvorticity ribbon on the leading edge of the mid-level trough, \naccompanied by the passage of the mid-level axis and a secondary\ncold front. This boundary will usher in cooler, much drier air,\nbringing a swift end to the rain.\n\nOverall rainfall will range from a half-inch to just over an \ninch across the event, though some spotty locations may miss out\nor only see light drizzle and brief showers. While slow-moving \nsurface boundaries and mid-level flow could encourage training \nand moderate rainfall at times, significant flooding is unlikely\ndue to ongoing drought conditions. Once this system exits, \ncompletely dry conditions will prevail for at least a week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A cold air wedge builds in for the weekend. \nAfter that, crisp fall weather with cool temperatures. \n\nA wedge pattern begins to develop Saturday and becomes more \npronounced through Sunday, eventually being eroded by the large \npush of high pressure and cool, dry air behind the secondary \nfront Monday. Temperatures will drop into the 60s and 70s \nareawide Saturday through the next work week. A slight warming \ntrend brings Wednesday so we should see fewer 60s and more 70s \nfor the end of the work week. Sunny skies will go some way to \nmaking it more of a pleasant fall weather stretch. here may be a\nnight or two where lows slip into the 30s in the mountain \nvalleys with patchy frost possible.",
